From 7364aa59a59fedf55be79d04ba498eb3c7308de3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aamir <aamir@tallow-lang.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 04:55:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] locator: the live scan takes its egress plane from the
 caller, and refuses without one
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`HttpWatcher::ureq_transport()` built a bare `ureq::get`, which the tree's egress guard
bans because it leaves from the box IP: the crate is a workspace member whose only outward
dependency is `tallow-registry`, so it cannot reach `runtime::egress_http` and the usual fix
is unavailable. The transport was already an injected seam; the defect was the locator
wiring a direct one itself.

So the locator no longer owns egress. `proxied_transport()` takes the proxy to ride from
TALLOW_LOCATOR_PROXY and fails closed when it is unset or unusable — a refusal, never a
silent fallback to a direct connection. `--live` then names that refusal per provider, and
the fail-close path prints why each watcher was dark: "no egress plane configured" and "no
API token" are different problems, and the generic "all watchers dark" line cannot tell them
apart.

Verified three ways. `cargo test -p tallow-locator` 20 passed (three new tests, written
against an env-independent core so no process env is mutated); the guard's
`no_bare_ureq_calls_in_the_runtime_tree` and `..._outside_the_egress_client` both pass, with
no bare ureq shape left in the crate; and the real binary end-to-end — with no proxy it
refuses before any request (exit 3, "TALLOW_LOCATOR_PROXY is not set — the locator will not
egress from the box IP" ×4), while with the box's Tor SOCKS proxy named it proceeds past the
egress gate and stops at the missing provider token, proving the refusal is the egress gate
and not a general failure.
